{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":21685041,"ns":0,"title":"The Polished Hoe","revisions":[{"revid":1333180865,"parentid":1295008556,"minor":false,"timestamp":"2026-01-16T05:21:38Z","size":10454,"sha1":"a1ae6d694f1979632c6f3f50f1e40ee5fa6f2ca7","slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","content":"{{short description|2002 novel by Austin Clarke}}\n{{Infobox book\n| name         = The Polished Hoe\n| image        = The Polished Hoe book cover.jpg\n| caption      =\n| author       = [[Austin Clarke (novelist)|Austin Clarke]]\n| cover_artist =\n| country      = Canada\n| language     = English\n| series       =\n| genre        =\n| publisher    = [[Thomas Allen Publishers]]\n| release_date = 2002\n| pages        =\n| isbn         = 0-88762-110-4\n| dewey        =\n| congress     =\n| oclc         =\n}}\n\n'''''The Polished Hoe''''' is a novel by Barbadian writer [[Austin Clarke (novelist)|Austin Clarke]], published by [[Thomas Allen Publishers]] in 2002. It was the winner of the 2002 [[Scotiabank Giller Prize]] and the 2003 [[Commonwealth Foundation prizes#Commonwealth Writers' Prize|Commonwealth Writers' Prize]] for Canada and the Caribbean region and 2003 [[Trillium Book Award]].\n\n== Plot ==\nThe novel is a narrative by Mary-Mathilda (Miss Mary Gertrude Matilda Paul) of her confession of a crime. The events takes place in about twenty-four hours of span starting on a night in the 1950s post [[World War II]] era. She is a respected woman of the island of [[Barbados]], popularly called \"Bimshire.\" She goes to the police and encounters her old friend Percy, a police sergeant with whom she shares unrequited feelings. Mary-Mathilda confesses to murdering Mr. Belfeels, the owner of a sugar plantation, a rich man known for his arrogance towards the workers under him. Mary-Mathilda had been working as a field labourer, kitchen help and then as a maid and for many years has also been Belfeels' mistress. She has a son Wilberforce with Belfeels who becomes a doctor after being funded by his father. Her son returns to the island after his studies abroad. Belfeels lives with his wife and two daughters and keeps Mary-Mathilda in a house on the outskirts of plantation away from the town. Belfeels objectifies her and treats her ruthlessly on various occasions. On their first encounter, while she was quite young, he undresses her using a [[Crop (implement)|riding crop]] while her mother turns a blind eye. Due to this she develops a nausea of leather's smell. She also discovers a dark secret kept by her mother that she herself is Belfeels' daughter which shatters her and provokes her to eventually murder Belfeels.\n\n== Publishing and development ==\nThe book is Clarke's tenth novel.<ref name=\"TG\"/> He had also published five short story collections before this book.<ref name=\"CBC\">{{cite web|url=http://www.cbc.ca/books/2013/08/2002.html|title=2002: The Polished Hoe by Austin Clarke|publisher=CBC Books|date=7 August 2013|accessdate=25 May 2017}}</ref> Clarke mentioned in an interview that he listened \"very attentively\" to American jazz trumpeter, bandleader, and composer [[Miles Davis]] while writing some of the sections of the novel.<ref>{{cite book|author=Donna Bailey Nurse|title=What's a Black Critic to Do?: Interviews, Profiles and Reviews of Black Writers|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=GeOSQT_O35gC&pg=PT135|year=2009|publisher=Insomniac Press|isbn=978-1-897414-53-8|page=209}}</ref>{{rp|135}} Clarke structured the novel in the form of short stories.<ref name=\"PBS\">{{cite web|url=https://www.pbs.org/newshour/show/the-polished-hoe|title='The Polished Hoe'|publisher=PBS|author=Jeffrey Brown|date=5 September 2003|access-date=25 May 2017}}</ref> The novel was published by [[Thomas Allen Publishers]] in November 2002.<ref name=\"QQ\"/>\n\nClarke mentioned that he was inspired by British poet and writer [[Geoffrey Chaucer]]'s ''[[The Canterbury Tales]]'', a collection of twenty-four stories that runs to over 17,000 lines written in [[Middle English]] and its \"non-traditional\" usage.<ref name=\"Clarke2015\">{{cite book|author=Austin Clarke|title=The Austin Clarke Library: 'Membering / The Polished Hoe / Choosing His Coffin|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=bQzxCQAAQBAJ|date=22 August 2015|publisher=Dundurn|isbn=978-1-4597-3440-1}}</ref> He also noted that \"[his] main concern was to find a language, or to more strictly use the language [he] already knew, in such a way that it become, in [his] manipulation of it, a ''new'' language.\"<ref name=\"Clarke2015\"/> Clarke said that he intended to \"creolize Oxford English\".<ref name=\"Clarke2015\"/>\n\nOne of the narratives of the novel is based on a real life incident in Clarke's life, which he describes as \"first confrontations with racism\". Clarke experienced racism while travelling from Barbados to Little England, Canada with the [[Trans-Canada Air Lines]] in September 1955. He narrated the incident as the journey told by Mary-Mathilda, the leading character of the novel, who is travelling from Miami to Buffalo. The novel was later translated into Dutch by publishing house, De Geus.<ref name=\"Clarke2015\"/>\n\nIn 2005, when Clarke read ''[[De Inventione]]'', an Italian handbook by [[Cicero|Marcus Tullius Cicero]], he noted that if he had known about it while writing the novel, \"[he] might very well have drawn the characters of Mary-Mathilda; her son, Wilberforce; Sergeant; and the Constable; to say nothing of Mr. Bellfeels; in sharper poignancy and focus\".<ref name=\"Clarke2015\"/> In the handbook, Cicero mentions that \"We hold the following to be the attributes of persons: name, nature, manner of life, fortune, habit, interests, purposes, achievements, accidents, conversation.\"<ref name=\"Clarke2015\"/>\n\nIn September 2022, [[Dundurn Press]] released a [https://www.dundurn.com/books_/t22117/a9781459750975-the-polished-hoe twentieth anniversary edition] of the novel with a foreword by [[Rinaldo Walcott]].\n\n== Reception and awards ==\nThe novel won the 2002 [[Scotiabank Giller Prize]] adjudged by [[Barbara Gowdy]], [[Thomas King (novelist)|Thomas King]], and [[W. H. New]] while being shortlisted along with ''Mount Appetite'' (by [[Bill Gaston]]), ''The Navigator of New York'' (by [[Wayne Johnston (author)|Wayne Johnston]]), ''Open'' (by [[Lisa Moore (writer)|Lisa Moore]]), and ''[[Unless]]'' (by [[Carol Shields]]).<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.scotiabankgillerprize.ca/about/past-winners/ |title= Past Winners and Juries |work= Scotiabank Giller Prize |accessdate=25 May 2017}}</ref> It also won the 2003 [[Commonwealth Foundation prizes#Commonwealth Writers' Prize|Commonwealth Writers' Prize]] for Canada and the Caribbean region and 2003 [[Trillium Book Award]].<ref name=\"DD\">{{cite web|url=https://www.dundurn.com/books/Polished-Hoe|title=The Polished Hoe @ Dundurn|publisher=Dundurn|accessdate=25 May 2017}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.omdc.on.ca/book/trillium_book_award/trillium_book_award_winners.htm|title=Trillium Book Award Winners|publisher=Ontario Media Development Corporation|accessdate=30 May 2017}}</ref>\n\nUpon publication, the novel received mostly favourable reviews with some criticism.<ref name=\"NY\">{{cite magazine|url=http://www.newyorker.com/magazine/2003/08/04/the-polished-hoe|title=The Polished Hoe|magazine=The New Yorker|date=4 August 2003|accessdate=25 May 2017}}</ref><ref name=\"Muse\">{{cite journal |author=Walcott-Hackshaw |first=Elizabeth |author-link=Elizabeth Walcott-Hackshaw |year=2006 |title=The Polished Hoe (review) |url=https://muse.jhu.edu/article/201551 |journal=Callaloo |publisher= |volume=29 |issue=2 |pages=680–682 |doi=10.1353/cal.2006.0103 |s2cid=161215737 |via=Project MUSE |accessdate=25 May 2017|url-access=subscription }}</ref><ref name=\"BB\">{{cite web|url=https://www.bookbrowse.com/reviews/index.cfm/book_number/1244/the-polished-hoe|title=The Polished Hoe|publisher=Book Browse|accessdate=25 May 2017}}</ref><ref name=\"HC\">{{cite web|url= http://www.chron.com/life/article/The-Polished-Hoe-by-Austin-Clarke-2126697.php|title='The Polished Hoe' by Austin Clarke: Conflict of colonialism at novel's heart|work=chron|author=Latha Viswanathan|date=17 August 2003|accessdate=25 May 2017}}</ref> ''[[Kirkus Reviews]]'' appreciated the novel as \"a memorable landscape of oppression but a problematic central figure\".<ref name=\"KR\">{{cite web|url=https://www.kirkusreviews.com/book-reviews/austin-clarke/the-polished-hoe/|title=The Polished Hoe|work=Kirkus Reviews|date=1 May 2003|accessdate=25 May 2017}}</ref> While Ihsan Taylor of ''The New York Times'' noted that \"There's a mesmerizing stillness to Austin Clarke's latest novel\",<ref name=\"NYT\">{{cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2003/09/14/books/mary-mathilda-s-murderous-monologue.html|title=Mary-Mathilda's Murderous Monologue|newspaper=The New York Times|author=Ihsan Taylor|date=14 September 2003|accessdate=25 May 2017}}</ref> British writer [[Maya Jaggi]] mentions in her ''The Guardian'' review that the \"[novel]'s meandering orality, its slow-burning power, succeed movingly in asserting memory over the silent gaps in recorded history.\"<ref name=\"TG\">{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/books/2004/apr/03/featuresreviews.guardianreview4|title=Swimming with barracudas|newspaper=The Guardian|author=Maya Jaggi|date=3 April 2004|accessdate=25 May 2017}}</ref> Another British novelist [[Naeem Murr]] criticised Clarke for losing confidence in his characters and for forcing them deliver sociological truths but appreciates the \"brilliantly written dialogue\".<ref name=\"PW\">{{cite web|url=https://www.publishersweekly.com/978-0-7432-3795-6|title=The Book Review: The Polished Hoe|work=Publishers Weekly|author=Naeem Murr|date=1 May 2003|accessdate=25 May 2017}}</ref> Craig Taylor in his ''Quill & Quire'' review called the novel as \"a wonderful book to meander\" and mentioned that \"it adheres to the slow pace of the life it describes and allows characters room to become memorable.\"<ref name=\"QQ\">{{cite web|url=http://www.quillandquire.com/review/the-polished-hoe/|title=Book Review: The Polished Hoe|work=Quill & Quire|date=20 February 2004|accessdate=29 May 2017}}</ref> [[Jeffrey Brown (journalist)|Jeffrey Brown]] of [[PBS]] noted in an interview with Clarke: \"There is a mix in your book of a kind of... I would call it almost a high poetic language, a very formal language, and then a dialect, the way they would talk to each other.\"<ref name=\"PBS\"/>\n\n== References ==\n{{Reflist|30em}}\n\n{{Giller Prize}}\n\n{{Authority control}}\n\n{{DEFAULTSORT:Polished Hoe, The}}\n[[Category:2002 Canadian novels]]\n[[Category:Novels by Austin Clarke (novelist)]]\n[[Category:Books about Barbados]]\n[[Category:Giller Prize–winning works]]\n[[Category:Barbados in World War II]]\n[[Category:Thomas Allen Publishers books]]"}}}],"contentmodel":"wikitext","pagelanguage":"en","pagelanguagehtmlcode":"en","pagelanguagedir":"ltr","touched":"2026-10-30T05:03:41Z","lastrevid":1333180865,"length":10454}]}}